meta {
  name: Insert Inventory Item
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/inventory
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Authorization: Bearer {{adminAccessToken}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "skuCode": "{{productId}}",
    "quantity": 50
  }
}

script:post-response {
  if (res.body.skuCode) {
    bru.setVar("inventorySkuCode", res.body.skuCode);
  }
}

docs {
  Requires product-service/Create Product.bru and auth-service/Login Admin.bru to have
  run first — needs {{productId}} and {{adminAccessToken}}.

  IMPORTANT — skuCode here MUST be the product's Mongo id ({{productId}}), not a
  human-readable code like "TECLADO-MECH-001" (used only as a Swagger @Schema example
  in InventoryRequestDTO, never enforced). order-service/Create Order.bru calls
  OrderService.createOrder(), which builds
  InventoryItemDTO(orderItem.productId(), orderItem.quantity()) — i.e. it sends the
  product id as the skuCode field when it calls inventory-service's reserve/release
  endpoints. Insert with any other skuCode and Create Order will fail with 409 /
  InsufficientStockException because inventory-service finds no matching row.

  Through "Gateway", POST /api/inventory requires ROLE_ADMIN
  (JwtAuthenticationFilter.requiresAdmin — matches every POST under /api/inventory,
  including /reserve and /release, see Reserve Stock.bru). Through "InventoryDirect"
  (:8083) the token is ignored entirely — inventory-service has no security config, so
  an unauthenticated POST also succeeds.

  InventoryDTO has no id field in its response — the numeric DB id (needed by
  Update Inventory Item.bru) isn't returned by any endpoint. See that request's docs.

  Expected: 201 CREATED with {skuCode, quantity, inStock: true}.
}
